u}$, where a neutrino and its antiparticle are produced, has been developed using data collected at Fermilab by the E949 experiment. The method involves tracking $K^+$ mesons as they slow down and analyzing $\pi^+$ mesons produced as they come to a halt. Utilizing an effective $K^+\pi^+$ momentum range of 470 to 690 MeV/$c$, and incorporating data from 1997 to 1999 with a total integrated luminosity of 2.88 pb$^{-1}$, a search for the decay process with the decay-in-motion method resulted in a null result. The frequency of occurrence for the decay process $K^+ightarrow\pi^+
u ar{
u}$ was found to be less than 6.0 x 10$^{-4}$ with 90% confidence, and the observed frequency of occurrence was estimated to be consistent with the prediction of the Standard Model, specifically with a branching ratio of 3.17 x 10$^{-11}$ with a margin of error.
